**nftables `ip filter` FORWARD chain on router6:**
```
iifname "eth2" ip saddr 172.16.10.0/24 ip daddr 172.18.51.1 tcp dport 80 … accept  ← narrow exception
iifname "eth2" meta l4proto tcp … drop           ← blocks all TCP from router3
iifname "eth2" meta l4proto udp … drop           ← blocks all UDP from router3 (incl. RIP)
iifname "eth2" counter packets 18 bytes 1512 drop ← catches all remaining (ICMP, etc.) — 18 pkts hit
```

- eth2 (IP 10.50.1.2/24) is the `r6_r3` link — the only physical path between router6 and router3
- All three drop rules target `iifname "eth2"` — traffic **arriving** at router6 from router3 side
- 18 packets / 1512 bytes already dropped (live counter) — confirming active traffic loss
- Route table shows router6 reaches ALL remote subnets (172.16.10.x, 172.18.50.x, 192.168.200.x, etc.) **via eth2 / 10.50.1.1** learned over RIP — but those RIP UDP updates are also blocked on eth2, so routes will stale once the hold-down timer expires

**Mechanism:** The block is unidirectional. Outbound traffic from router6 → router3 is unaffected, so ICMP echo *requests* from pc15–pc18 reach their destinations (pc12, pc14). The echo *replies* travel back router4 → router3 → router6-eth2 and are discarded by the catch-all drop rule, producing the confirmed ICMP timeout.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: nftables FORWARD chain on router6, interface eth2 (the r6_r3 physical link, 10.50.1.2/24 — the sole interconnect between router6 and router3).

Mechanism: Three drop rules have been injected into router6's ip filter FORWARD chain, all keyed on `iifname "eth2"`. Rule 1 drops all TCP arriving from router3; rule 2 drops all UDP (which also silently kills incoming RIP updates); rule 3 is a protocol-agnostic catch-all that drops every remaining packet, including ICMP. One narrow `accept` exception permits HTTP from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, but everything else is discarded. The live drop counter already shows 18 packets / 1512 bytes absorbed. Because the rules match only inbound direction (`iifname`), traffic flowing router6 → router3 leaves normally; only the return traffic is silently discarded.

Localization: router6, eth2. All other interfaces on router6, and all other devices, are clean.

Root cause: Rogue nftables rules were applied to the FORWARD hook on router6's eth2 interface, making the r6_r3 link unidirectional and silently dropping all return traffic from the router3 segment. This breaks every cross-network flow that relies on router3 routing replies back through router6 — most visibly, pc15–pc18 (172.18.51.x, behind mswitch2/switch10) cannot receive responses from destinations reachable via router3 such as pc12/pc14 (192.168.200.x). The same block also corrupts any RIP convergence that depends on router3 sending updates to router6 over that link.

Fix: Flush the three drop rules from router6's FORWARD chain for iifname "eth2": `nft flush chain ip filter FORWARD` (or surgically delete the three drop rules while preserving any legitimate accepts), then verify RIP reconverges on router6's eth2 routes.
=== END ===